---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Navbar from '../components/Navbar.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';

const title = 'About Us | Cinematic Photo Editor';
const description = 'Learn about Cinematic Photo, a free, client-side online photo editor that applies movie-inspired color grades, film grain, and letterboxing entirely in your browser.';
---

<Layout title={title} description={description} path="/about-us/">
	<Navbar />
	<main class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-20 sm:px-6">
		<span class="mb-4 block font-mono text-xs tracking-wide text-mute">ABOUT US</span>
		<h1 class="font-sans text-3xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.03em] text-ink sm:text-4xl">
			A cinematic photo editor built by people who love film color
		</h1>

		<div class="mt-8 flex flex-col gap-6 text-base leading-7 text-body">
			<p>
				Cinematic Photo started from a simple frustration: getting a photo to look like a still
				from a favorite movie usually meant hand-tuning curves in a desktop editor, hunting down
				presets of unclear origin, or paying for a subscription just to try a handful of color
				grades. We wanted something faster: a tool that goes straight from a flat photo to a
				movie-grade look in one click, without a learning curve in the way.
			</p>
			<p>
				So we built a focused, browser-based editor around a small set of color grades modeled on
				recognizable film looks (teal-and-orange neo-noir, cold cosmic blue, warm desert gold,
				high-gain digital green, and crushed-shadow steel), plus the texture details that sell a
				cinematic still: film grain, vignette, glow and halation, anamorphic letterboxing, and a
				film-stock stamp.
			</p>
			<h2 class="font-sans text-xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink">Why it's private by default</h2>
			<p>
				Every filter, grade, and export in Cinematic Photo runs locally on an HTML canvas inside
				your own browser tab. We made this choice deliberately: your photos are yours, and a
				color-grading tool has no real reason to see them. There are no uploads, no accounts, and
				no server-side storage. The editing happens entirely on your device.
			</p>
			<h2 class="font-sans text-xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em] text-ink">Who we build for</h2>
			<p>
				Photographers, filmmakers, and social creators use Cinematic Photo for thumbnails,
				posters, and portfolio stills. If that sounds like you and you run into a rough edge or
				want to see a new preset, we'd like to hear about it. Reach out on our <a href="/contact-us" class="text-ink underline decoration-hairline-strong underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-ink">Contact Us</a> page.
			</p>
		</div>
	</main>
	<Footer />
</Layout>
